# Normalise line endings in the repository, regardless of what the committer's
# core.autocrlf happens to be. Without this, a Windows checkout rewrites every
# .go file to CRLF in the working tree, and gofmt then reports the whole file as
# changed even where nothing is -- which is indistinguishable from a real
# formatting problem and gets ignored until it hides an actual one.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The Windows packaging scripts are run by PowerShell and NSIS on Windows, and
# the NSIS preprocessor is whitespace sensitive around its quoted batch lines.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.nsi text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary assets. Never let git try to normalise these.
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.deb binary
*.msi binary
*.db binary
